  "documents": [
    {
      "page_content": "Clothing Management System categorizes items into CAT-I and CAT-II. CAT-I comprises personal clothing items issued directly to individuals for personal wear and life-cycle retention (e.g., combat shirts, uniforms, trousers, socks, berets, badges, rank insignias). CAT-II comprises unit-held communal and tactical equipment retained on unit inventory charge (e.g., heavy weather parkas, combat helmets, tents, specialized high-altitude boots, load-bearing vests). CAT-II items must be returned to unit quartermaster stores when operations conclude or personnel transfer.",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "doc_cat_policies",
        "title": "CMS Clothing Categories: CAT-I vs CAT-II Policy",
        "category": "policy",
        "source": "CMS Standard Operating Procedures"
      }
    },
    {
      "page_content": "Key inventory metrics in CMS:\n- AUTH (Authorized Scale): The official mandated quantity of an item a unit or formation is permitted to hold.\n- Held (Stock Held): Active equipment currently present in unit warehouse, split into SER (Serviceable) and PWS (Partially Working State).\n- Res (Reserve Stock): Central backup pool at Division or DOU, split into Res SER and Res PWS.\n- Total Stock: Sum of Reserve Stock (SER + PWS) and Stock Held (SER + PWS).\n- Deficiency (DEFI): The deficit calculated as Authorized Scale minus Total Stock Held (DEFI = AUTH - Held). A positive deficiency indicates missing equipment that must be demanded from SCME.",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "doc_stock_terminology",
        "title": "CMS Inventory Terminology: AUTH, Held, Reserve, Deficiency",
        "category": "inventory",
        "source": "CMS Inventory Manual"
      }
    },
    {
      "page_content": "The CMS organizational tree is structured as ORD -> Division -> Brigade -> Unit.\n- ORD (Super Admin): System administrator managing the overall organization tree, user creation, and top-level analytics.\n- Division Admin: Oversees subordinate brigades and units, manages Division Reserve Stock (Res SER/PWS), and issues stock to units.\n- Brigade Admin: Monitors brigade-level readiness, aggregate unit inventory, and supply flow.\n- Unit Admin / Quartermaster: Tracks unit-held items, logs personnel strength, monitors deficiencies, and submits item demands.\n- SCME: Central equipment depot and catalog manager responsible for introducing catalog items, adding bulk stock, and issuing monthly ECC statements.",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "doc_hierarchy_roles",
        "title": "CMS Military Hierarchy and User Role Responsibilities",
        "category": "hierarchy",
        "source": "CMS User Guide"
      }
    },
    {
      "page_content": "Items in the CMS catalog are counted by specific Accounting Units (A/U):\n- Nos (Numbers / Units): Single individual items such as combat jackets, shirts, helmets, berets, web belts, altimeters.\n- Prs (Pairs): Items supplied and issued in matched sets of two, such as combat boots, wool socks, tactical gloves, insoles.\nAll inventory statements, issue vouchers, and demand slips must specify the correct A/U to prevent quantity mismatches.",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "doc_accounting_units",
        "title": "Accounting Units (A/U): Nos vs Prs",
        "category": "catalog",
        "source": "CMS Catalog Standards"
      }
    },
    {
      "page_content": "The Productive Analysis graph is a 3D Grouped Bar Chart comparing three quantities side-by-side for each unit:\n1. Authorized Scale (what the unit is mandated to have),\n2. Held (what the unit currently holds in stores),\n3. Deficiency (shortfall: Authorized - Held).\nManagers use this chart to rapidly identify units suffering the widest equipment deficits across key equipment items.",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "doc_graph_productive_analysis",
        "title": "Dashboard Graph 1: Productive Analysis (3D Grouped Bar Chart)",
        "category": "graphs",
        "source": "graph_readme.md"
      }
    },
    {
      "page_content": "The Global Fulfillment graph is a 3D circular Donut Chart displaying overall system-wide stock availability.\nIt computes the percentage of total authorized clothing currently held across all formations: (Total Held / Total Auth) * 100%.\nThe center percentage indicates base-wide health, with green representing stocked goods and red representing unfulfilled deficiencies.",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "doc_graph_global_fulfillment",
        "title": "Dashboard Graph 2: Global Fulfillment (3D Donut Chart)",
        "category": "graphs",
        "source": "graph_readme.md"
      }
    },
    {
      "page_content": "The Top 5 Deficient Items graph ranks the 5 specific clothing articles with the highest cumulative shortages system-wide.\nItems with the longest bars represent urgent procurement priorities for SCME supply officers to restock first.",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "doc_graph_top_deficiencies",
        "title": "Dashboard Graph 3: Top 5 Deficient Items (3D Horizontal Bar Chart)",
        "category": "graphs",
        "source": "graph_readme.md"
      }
    },
    {
      "page_content": "The Deficiency Share by Unit graph is a 3D Pie Chart showing the percentage proportion of total system shortage that belongs to each individual military unit.\nIf Unit A accounts for 50% of the pie, half of the total missing equipment in the formation is concentrated in Unit A.",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "doc_graph_deficiency_share",
        "title": "Dashboard Graph 4: Deficiency Share by Unit (3D Pie Chart)",
        "category": "graphs",
        "source": "graph_readme.md"
      }
    },
    {
      "page_content": "The A/U Split chart categorizes held stock by unit of measurement: Nos (individual items like jackets, helmets) versus Prs (pairs like boots, gloves).\nIt provides supply chain logistics insight into handling single-piece garments versus paired accessories.",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "doc_graph_au_split",
        "title": "Dashboard Graph 5: Accounting Unit Quantity Split (3D Column Chart)",
        "category": "graphs",
        "source": "graph_readme.md"
      }
    },
    {
      "page_content": "Supply Readiness is calculated per unit as (Held / Authorized) * 100% and rendered in 3D color-coded columns:\n- Green (>= 80%): Excellent readiness / fully operational.\n- Amber (50% - 79%): Warning state / needs replenishment.\n- Red (< 50%): Critical deficiency / urgent supply action required.",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "doc_graph_readiness",
        "title": "Dashboard Graph 6: Supply Readiness by Unit (3D Color-Coded Column Chart)",
        "category": "graphs",
        "source": "graph_readme.md"
      }
    },
    {
      "page_content": "CMS relational database schema includes:\n- clothing_items: Catalog defining item name, category (CAT-I, CAT-II), accounting unit (Nos, Prs), and specifications.\n- inventory: Warehouse stock tracking held quantities (SER, PWS) per unit and item.\n- units: Organizational hierarchy nodes (Division, Brigade, Unit).\n- users: System user credentials, ranks, and assigned roles (ORD, Division Admin, Unit Admin, SCME).\n- demands: Electronic replenishment requests raised by units.\n- sales / issuances: Records of clothing issued or sold to personnel.\n- authorizations: Authorized scale limits configured per unit and item.",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "doc_schema_tables",
        "title": "CMS Database Architecture and Usable Tables",
        "category": "schema",
        "source": "CMS Database Architecture"
      }
    },
    {
      "page_content": "The AI Assistant and CMS dashboards support multi-format exports for session data:\n- CSV: Raw comma-separated data suitable for data analysis scripts.\n- Excel (XLS): Tab-separated spreadsheet format compatible with Microsoft Excel and Google Sheets.\n- PDF / HTML: Print-ready formatted document with headers, table borders, and CMS metadata.\nUsers can request 'Export data as Excel' or click export action buttons.",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "doc_export_formats",
        "title": "CMS Data Export Capabilities (CSV, Excel, PDF)",
        "category": "features",
        "source": "CMS Feature Manual"
      }
    }
  ],
